---
import Film from '../../plate/Film.astro';
import { renderFormula } from '../../../lib/formulas/render';
import { CUES, TIMELINE } from './story';
import './style.css';

const labels = [
  { id: 'colour-half', tex: String.raw`p=\frac12` },
  { id: 'mesh-limit', tex: String.raw`\varepsilon\longrightarrow0` },
  { id: 'cross-ratio', tex: String.raw`x\in(0,1)` },
  { id: 'boundary-map', tex: String.raw`a,c,d\longmapsto0,1,\infty` },
  { id: 'cardy-function', tex: String.raw`F(x)` },
  { id: 'cardy-formula', tex: String.raw`F(x)=\frac{\int_0^x[u(1-u)]^{-2/3}\,du}{\int_0^1[u(1-u)]^{-2/3}\,du}` },
  { id: 'cardy-square', tex: String.raw`F(0.5)=0.5` },
  { id: 'cardy-wide', tex: String.raw`F(x_{\mathrm{wide}})\simeq0.1756` },
  { id: 'cardy-tall', tex: String.raw`F(x_{\mathrm{tall}})\simeq0.8244` },
  { id: 'crossing-limit', tex: String.raw`\mathbb P(\mathcal C_\varepsilon)\longrightarrow F(x)` },
  { id: 'pivotal-law', tex: String.raw`\mathbb E N_{\mathrm{piv}}\sim c\,\varepsilon^{-3/4}` },
] as const;
---

<Film
  tag="uruk-random-mosaic"
  cues={CUES}
  timeline={TIMELINE}
  step={50}
  labels={labels}
  description="Eighty-seven retained sites grow into a square Voronoi mosaic. Forty-six cells turn black while forty-one remain paper. A ten-cell chain crosses the finite picture from left to right. The window expands with additional illustrative sites into a wide rectangle, rotates into a tall rectangle, and returns to the same square while the sites retain their colour identities. The marked sides then travel onto a half-line and a Cardy-function plot. Three finite raster-sampling frequencies are drawn separately from the formula values. Blue rings, a bracket and tags identify the papers' claimed annealed limiting law and conditional companion results. Forty stored trial outcomes illustrate that annealed probability averages over new mosaics and new colours. The closing ledger preserves the dated absence of human and machine checking."
  caption="A finite black-and-white Voronoi mosaic carries a crossing question into Cardy's parameter plot. Stored toy frequencies are compared with the formula. Blue marks identify the papers' claimed limiting law; the finite pictures do not test that claim."
>
  <p class="film-method">How the film is made. The example retains the direction's seeded finite-window data: eighty-seven sites and forty-six black cells. The browser computes a ten-cell left-right chain by breadth-first search through actual shared-edge polygon adjacency. The browser constructs the Voronoi outlines of the retained sites. The drawing grows cells from their sites and fills their prescribed colours; this animation does not resample the random process. Additional sites used while the window expands are an illustrative continuation, not one of the stored measurement trials. Site identities and their colours travel with the picture.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The measured toy uses points sampled only inside a finite window, with about eighty sites per unit area. It omits outside sites whose full-plane Voronoi cells could enter the window. Regions and adjacency for the stored trial outcomes are approximated on a raster with forty-eight pixels per unit length. A black crossing is found by searching the resulting cell-adjacency graph. The displayed polygon outlines and the raster computation serve different purposes: the outlines and their shared-edge adjacency supply the carried example and its ten-cell crossing, while the raster outcome strings supply the reported frequencies. These small-window, finite-resolution outcomes are not exact samples of the full-plane crossing event in the theorem.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The square has four hundred retained left-right outcomes, with frequency <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`0.445`, false)} />. A separate run of four hundred wide-rectangle mosaics gives left-right frequency <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`0.1875`, false)} /> and top-bottom frequency <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`0.78`, false)} />. Rotating the same wide mosaics through ninety degrees turns their top-bottom crossings into the tall rectangle's left-right crossings. The wide and tall readouts therefore share mosaics. They are not independent runs. The shape changes of the single carried example illustrate these crossing questions rather than reproduce any measured trial.</p>
  <p class="film-method">Each whisker has half-width <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`0.025=\frac{1}{2\sqrt{400}}`, false)} />, the maximum binomial standard error for four hundred independent outcomes. It is not a confidence interval or a rigorous error bound. The square's observed frequency sits below one half; the cause of that discrepancy in this toy has not been investigated. Its finite-window and raster approximations prevent the comparison from testing Cardy's limiting formula. The first forty retained square outcomes form the later tick board; the mosaic left on screen remains the one example.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The Cardy curve is the integral formula quoted in the article. The rectangle's conformal cross-ratio is calculated through the Schwarz-Christoffel relation and elliptic integrals. The square, wide two-to-one rectangle and tall one-to-two rectangle have cross-ratios approximately <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`0.5,\ 0.0294,\ 0.9706`, false)} /> and formula values approximately <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`0.5,\ 0.1756,\ 0.8244`, false)} />, respectively. The toy bars use their actual frequencies at these same horizontal parameters. Hollow toy markers are displaced a few pixels horizontally for legibility, with leaders to their actual cross-ratios. Neither the curve nor the cross-ratio is fitted to those frequencies. The half-line transition carries four boundary marks; it is a schematic illustration of a conformal map, not a numerical conformal map of the drawn cells.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The half-density gauge cites Bollobás and Riordan's 2006 critical-point theorem, rather than deriving it from the one crossing shown. The compass rotates a finite picture to illustrate the absence of a preferred direction in the full-plane Poisson law; one rotated sample does not establish rotation or conformal invariance. Smirnov's 2001 triangular-site result is a separate known result and remains in ink.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The main blue claim concerns the annealed crossing probability for the full-plane Poisson-Voronoi model at fair colour density, in every bounded Jordan domain with four distinct boundary marks, as point intensity tends to infinity. Annealed means averaging over both the mosaic and its colours. The companion near-critical statement is conditional on the tessellation, concerns a joint law for rational polygonal quadrilaterals and normalizes each model by its own mean pivotal count. The pivotal-count companion gives a positive-constant asymptotic proportional to <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`\varepsilon^{-3/4}`, false)} />. These companions take the main theorem as input. The main result does not assert an SLE interface limit, a full CLE limit or a quenched crossing limit for arbitrary Jordan domains.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The closing ledger keeps the article's unreviewed status and evidence date, 20 October 2026. Family 224 has no Lean scope note or comparator statement in the pinned release, and the film performs no machine proof check. With JavaScript disabled, the article and transcript remain available without a fallback illustration. Reduced motion selects one completed supporting frame for each of the eleven cues.</p>
</Film>

<script>
  import './element';
</script>
